WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.330 --> 00:00:01.560
- [Narrator] Go through checkout,

00:00:01.560 --> 00:00:05.040
tap your card and there,

00:00:05.040 --> 00:00:08.010
you paid in less than two seconds.

00:00:08.010 --> 00:00:09.971
It's faster than, hold on,

00:00:09.971 --> 00:00:12.120
(machine blips)

00:00:12.120 --> 00:00:15.210
this and safer than this.

00:00:15.210 --> 00:00:18.720
That quick exchange is thanks
in large part to these,

00:00:18.720 --> 00:00:20.490
just a few little wires

00:00:20.490 --> 00:00:23.670
that make contactless payments possible.

00:00:23.670 --> 00:00:26.460
Here's a look inside the tech
that makes those payments

00:00:26.460 --> 00:00:30.120
both quick and secure and why tap-to-pay

00:00:30.120 --> 00:00:33.540
has taken so long to
gain traction in the US.

00:00:33.540 --> 00:00:36.183
This is the tech behind tap-to-pay.

00:00:38.004 --> 00:00:38.930
(machine beeps)

00:00:38.930 --> 00:00:42.870
At the heart of tap-to-pay
is a technology called NFC,

00:00:42.870 --> 00:00:47.730
or near field communication,
which itself is a specific form

00:00:47.730 --> 00:00:51.990
of RFID or radio frequency identification.

00:00:51.990 --> 00:00:53.280
You might be familiar with it

00:00:53.280 --> 00:00:56.460
as a way to unlock your
hotel room, for example.

00:00:56.460 --> 00:00:59.070
NFC relies on those little wires

00:00:59.070 --> 00:01:00.720
which are actually antennas.

00:01:00.720 --> 00:01:04.980
- The antenna looks like a
racetrack and it's a coil

00:01:04.980 --> 00:01:07.500
of different wires and that's what's used

00:01:07.500 --> 00:01:11.220
to basically transmit the
radio frequency on both sides.

00:01:11.220 --> 00:01:12.690
- [Narrator] This is Mike Maclennan.

00:01:12.690 --> 00:01:15.420
He's the general manager
of hardware at Square,

00:01:15.420 --> 00:01:17.430
a company that makes card readers.

00:01:17.430 --> 00:01:19.170
And he cracked one open to show us

00:01:19.170 --> 00:01:22.230
exactly how a tap-to-pay
transaction works.

00:01:22.230 --> 00:01:24.780
- Reminds me a lot of opening oysters.

00:01:24.780 --> 00:01:27.330
- [Narrator] Card readers
also have those antennas.

00:01:27.330 --> 00:01:29.670
- This is the NFC antenna,
so this is the magic

00:01:29.670 --> 00:01:32.340
of what makes NFC transactions
work and you can see,

00:01:32.340 --> 00:01:36.210
once we pull it off how
thin the NFC antenna is.

00:01:36.210 --> 00:01:37.410
- [Narrator] The card and the reader

00:01:37.410 --> 00:01:40.680
are what he calls passive
and active connections.

00:01:40.680 --> 00:01:43.800
The reader is active because it has power,

00:01:43.800 --> 00:01:47.040
and can initiate radio
communication on its own.

00:01:47.040 --> 00:01:50.790
And the card is passive
because it doesn't have power

00:01:50.790 --> 00:01:52.500
and needs to be near an active source

00:01:52.500 --> 00:01:54.570
to initiate that communication.

00:01:54.570 --> 00:01:56.310
Tapping your phone works the same way,

00:01:56.310 --> 00:01:59.040
because your phone
emulates the passive card.

00:01:59.040 --> 00:02:01.740
- What's happening right now
is the coil in here is active

00:02:01.740 --> 00:02:04.200
and it's looking for a card, basically,

00:02:04.200 --> 00:02:06.990
anywhere within this region up here.

00:02:06.990 --> 00:02:09.330
- [Narrator] It's doing
that using a radio frequency

00:02:09.330 --> 00:02:11.661
specific to NFC devices, which only works

00:02:11.661 --> 00:02:16.661
at a very short range, a maximum
of about four centimeters.

00:02:17.040 --> 00:02:18.960
And when the reader finds the card,

00:02:18.960 --> 00:02:21.780
it requests the information
needed for payment.

00:02:21.780 --> 00:02:25.530
That information is stored
here in your card's chip.

00:02:25.530 --> 00:02:28.260
One thing it stores is
what's known as static data,

00:02:28.260 --> 00:02:30.240
meaning information that's the same

00:02:30.240 --> 00:02:31.920
every time you use your card.

00:02:31.920 --> 00:02:35.280
That's things like the account
number and expiration date.

00:02:35.280 --> 00:02:37.980
It's what's sent from your
card's magnetic stripe

00:02:37.980 --> 00:02:39.090
when you swipe.

00:02:39.090 --> 00:02:41.970
But your chip doesn't
just send that static data

00:02:41.970 --> 00:02:45.420
when you tap, it also sends
something called a cryptogram,

00:02:45.420 --> 00:02:48.360
a unique string of numbers
the issuers use to verify

00:02:48.360 --> 00:02:49.860
that your card is valid.

00:02:49.860 --> 00:02:52.290
- What the card is doing is
it's collecting information

00:02:52.290 --> 00:02:55.680
from the reader about the transaction,

00:02:55.680 --> 00:02:58.620
about the reader specifically,
and it's combining that

00:02:58.620 --> 00:03:02.640
with the the card information
using its cryptographic key

00:03:02.640 --> 00:03:05.730
to create a unique number
for that transaction.

00:03:05.730 --> 00:03:08.100
That's what makes it
really hard to replicate

00:03:08.100 --> 00:03:10.800
a card fraudulently is
because the card needs

00:03:10.800 --> 00:03:13.080
to have a cryptographic key

00:03:13.080 --> 00:03:15.813
that lets them generate that secret code.

00:03:17.280 --> 00:03:19.710
- [Narrator] That information
is fed through this,

00:03:19.710 --> 00:03:22.080
the payment board which sorts to the data

00:03:22.080 --> 00:03:23.430
that needs from the card

00:03:23.430 --> 00:03:26.610
and puts it all into an encrypted package.

00:03:26.610 --> 00:03:29.340
That package then gets
sent off to the main board,

00:03:29.340 --> 00:03:30.330
which then.

00:03:30.330 --> 00:03:33.480
- Sends that on to our
servers, which then send it on

00:03:33.480 --> 00:03:36.690
to the networks like
Visa, MasterCard, AMEX,

00:03:36.690 --> 00:03:39.060
which then send it on to
the issuer and then back

00:03:39.060 --> 00:03:40.200
with the approval message.

00:03:40.200 --> 00:03:42.330
Once you get to this
board, it's all encrypted

00:03:42.330 --> 00:03:46.140
and protected information
and so anything beyond this

00:03:46.140 --> 00:03:49.440
will have a high degree of
security involved in it.

00:03:49.440 --> 00:03:52.140
- [Narrator] All of that
happens in just a few seconds,

00:03:52.140 --> 00:03:54.210
faster than inserting your chip.

00:03:54.210 --> 00:03:57.420
- So when you insert a
chip card into a reader,

00:03:57.420 --> 00:04:00.450
there's a few back and
forths in that communication

00:04:00.450 --> 00:04:03.090
whereas in contactless, there's
just fewer back and forth.

00:04:03.090 --> 00:04:05.010
It was just built as a more efficient,

00:04:05.010 --> 00:04:07.170
more streamlined, only
the bare necessities

00:04:07.170 --> 00:04:09.780
kind of information being
passed back and forth.

00:04:09.780 --> 00:04:12.480
- [Narrator] Card networks
and security experts say

00:04:12.480 --> 00:04:14.100
that this is also more secure

00:04:14.100 --> 00:04:16.440
than other ways you can use your card.

00:04:16.440 --> 00:04:18.450
Unlike swiping, your static data

00:04:18.450 --> 00:04:21.540
is better protected
during that communication.

00:04:21.540 --> 00:04:24.090
And because it doesn't have
contact with the reader,

00:04:24.090 --> 00:04:25.830
it's not susceptible to malware

00:04:25.830 --> 00:04:29.253
that can affect chip insert
transactions on rare occasions.

00:04:30.540 --> 00:04:33.090
This tap-to-pay technology has taken off

00:04:33.090 --> 00:04:35.850
in the last few years, in
part because of the push

00:04:35.850 --> 00:04:38.280
for contactless during the pandemic.

00:04:38.280 --> 00:04:40.470
Square says such payments tripled

00:04:40.470 --> 00:04:44.340
between the beginning of
2020 and the end of 2022.

00:04:44.340 --> 00:04:47.730
While other countries like
the UK have had contactless

00:04:47.730 --> 00:04:50.700
integrated into public
transportation for years,

00:04:50.700 --> 00:04:54.090
it's taken the US more
than a decade to get here.

00:04:54.090 --> 00:04:55.350
- [Announcer] For a whole new way to pay,

00:04:55.350 --> 00:04:57.360
MasterCard introduces Pay Pass.

00:04:57.360 --> 00:05:01.050
- US first started issuing
tap-to-pay cards in the 2000s.

00:05:01.050 --> 00:05:02.100
- [Narrator] Peter Rudegair

00:05:02.100 --> 00:05:03.780
is a Wall Street Journal reporter

00:05:03.780 --> 00:05:06.480
who covers payments and
financial technology.

00:05:06.480 --> 00:05:08.970
- Different banks thought it
was a cool, new technology

00:05:08.970 --> 00:05:11.310
and thought their consumers
would really flock to it.

00:05:11.310 --> 00:05:12.780
Didn't really work out just because

00:05:12.780 --> 00:05:14.250
there were few businesses that were set up

00:05:14.250 --> 00:05:16.080
to accept tap-to-pay.

00:05:16.080 --> 00:05:17.910
- [Narrator] US Retailers have been slow

00:05:17.910 --> 00:05:19.800
to replace payment systems,

00:05:19.800 --> 00:05:23.160
in part because they're
expensive to change.

00:05:23.160 --> 00:05:27.060
In 2022, about 55% of merchants said

00:05:27.060 --> 00:05:28.650
they accepted tap-to-pay,

00:05:28.650 --> 00:05:31.920
with others saying they were
planning on implementing it.

00:05:31.920 --> 00:05:35.010
Consumer behaviors are
also difficult to change

00:05:35.010 --> 00:05:38.520
and the switch to contactless
didn't feel urgent.

00:05:38.520 --> 00:05:40.140
- It's just often been described

00:05:40.140 --> 00:05:41.850
as a solution in search of a problem.

00:05:41.850 --> 00:05:44.520
Yes, it might be convenient
and yes, it might be safe,

00:05:44.520 --> 00:05:46.080
but that's not usually enough

00:05:46.080 --> 00:05:47.880
for people to switch their behavior.

00:05:48.810 --> 00:05:50.880
- [Narrator] The tech
still has room to grow

00:05:50.880 --> 00:05:52.140
and the push to integrate it

00:05:52.140 --> 00:05:55.980
into more parts of everyday
life is working in its favor.

00:05:55.980 --> 00:05:57.570
In New York, for example,

00:05:57.570 --> 00:06:00.930
subway riders can now
tap-to-pay at turnstiles.

00:06:00.930 --> 00:06:03.300
- So you have this big
push to get tap-to-pay

00:06:03.300 --> 00:06:05.280
into more transportation systems

00:06:05.280 --> 00:06:07.620
and to get people used
to using it every day.

00:06:07.620 --> 00:06:09.210
And if you're gonna use tap-to-pay

00:06:09.210 --> 00:06:11.100
to get on and off the
subway at least twice a day,

00:06:11.100 --> 00:06:13.530
maybe you'll also use it for your coffee,

00:06:13.530 --> 00:06:15.960
your lunch, your drinks.

00:06:15.960 --> 00:06:16.793
At least that's the idea

00:06:16.793 --> 00:06:18.690
that the credit card
companies hope happens.

00:06:18.690 --> 00:06:20.280
- [Narrator] Square and other companies

00:06:20.280 --> 00:06:22.740
have also recently released
to the functionality

00:06:22.740 --> 00:06:24.870
to use the NFC antenna in your phone

00:06:24.870 --> 00:06:28.260
as the active connection,
essentially putting a reader

00:06:28.260 --> 00:06:30.600
in your pocket, which could make it easier

00:06:30.600 --> 00:06:33.840
for small retailers to
accept card payments.

00:06:33.840 --> 00:06:35.670
- This is showing you
where it wants you to tap

00:06:35.670 --> 00:06:39.450
and so the NFC antenna on your
phone is right behind this.

00:06:39.450 --> 00:06:42.330
And so when I take my
NFC-enabled credit card

00:06:42.330 --> 00:06:47.330
and I hold it over there,
done, transaction complete.

00:06:50.397 --> 00:06:52.741
(bright music)

